# Caddyfile for the port-scanner tunnel edge.
#
# One hostname, one ordinary certificate. The edge routes by path — the first
# segment of the URL is the tunnel id (`/abc123/api/x` reaches agent abc123 as
# `/api/x`) — so there are no per-tunnel subdomains and nothing here needs a
# wildcard cert, a DNS-01 challenge, or a DNS-provider plugin. Stock Caddy from
# apt is enough; `xcaddy` is not.
#
# Caddy issues over HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN-01 on its own, which needs two things:
#   - ports 80 and 443 reachable from the internet
#   - an A record pointing straight at this VPS, not proxied by a CDN
#     (in Cloudflare terms: DNS-only / grey cloud, not orange)
#
# A `*.portscanner.kreativekorna.com` wildcard record also exists. Path routing
# does not use it, and it is deliberately not listed below: naming it here would
# make Caddy try to get a wildcard certificate, which only DNS-01 can satisfy —
# reintroducing the provider plugin and API token this config exists to avoid.
# The wildcard becomes relevant only if tunnels ever move to per-tunnel
# subdomains.
#
# Changing domains means editing the hostname below and BASE_DOMAIN in
# /etc/portscanner-edge.env; they must agree, because the edge builds the
# public URLs it hands to clients out of BASE_DOMAIN.
#
# reverse_proxy needs no extra configuration here: it upgrades WebSocket
# connections on /agent by itself, passes the original Host through to an
# HTTP upstream, and sets X-Forwarded-For/-Proto/-Host.

portscanner.kreativekorna.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8443
}
